       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks like an error.\n\n- **The order:** metformin 4,250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day.\n- **The limit:** the usual maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin. The FDA label allows up to 2,550 mg/day, and some references go as high as 3,000 mg/day. A single 4,250 mg dose is also well above the maximum for one administration (typically 1,000 mg).\n- **The risk:** a dose this high carries a serious risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nYou may have meant one of these:\n- **850 mg** three times daily (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **425 mg**, though metformin isn't made in that strength. Common tablets are 500, 850 and 1,000 mg.\n- **500 mg** two or three times daily, which is a more typical dose\n\nIf you're starting metformin, the usual approach is 500 mg once or twice daily with meals. Then increase it gradually based on tolerance and glycemic response.\n\nTell me the dose you intended and I'll enter it. Please also confirm the dose with your resident or attending first."
      }
